== Texts of Scripture Brought to Mind

V. It is no sign that religious affections are truly holy and spiritual, or that they are not, that they come with texts of Scripture remarkably brought to the mind.

It is no sign that affections are not gracious that they are occasioned by Scriptures coming to mind in this way, provided it is the Scripture itself, or the truth which the Scripture so brought contains and teaches, that is the foundation of the affection, and not merely or mainly the sudden and unusual manner of its coming to the mind.

But on the other hand, neither is it any sign that affections are gracious that they arise on the occasion of Scriptures brought suddenly and wonderfully to the mind, whether those affections are fear or hope, joy or sorrow, or any other. Some seem to look on this as good evidence that their affections are saving, especially if the affections stirred up are hope or joy, or any others that are pleasing and delightful. They will mention it as evidence that all is right that their experience #emph[came with the word.] They will say, “Such and such sweet promises were brought to my mind. They came suddenly, as if they were spoken to me. I had no hand in bringing such a text to my own mind. I was not thinking of anything leading to it. It came all at once, so that I was surprised. I had not thought of it for a long time before. I did not know at first that it was Scripture. I did not remember ever having read it.” And perhaps they will add, “One Scripture came flowing in after another, and so texts from all over the Bible, the sweetest and most pleasant, and the most fitting and suitable that could be devised. They filled me as full as I could hold. I could only stand and wonder. The tears flowed. I was full of joy and could not doubt any longer.” And so they think they have undoubted evidence that their affections must be from God and of the right kind, and that their state is good, but they have no grounds of any kind for it. Where did they get any such rule, that if any affections or experiences arise with promises and comforting texts of Scripture brought to mind in a way they cannot account for, without their calling them to mind, or if a great number of sweet texts follow one another in a chain, this is certain evidence that their experiences are saving? Where is any such rule to be found in the Bible, the great and only sure guide in things of this nature?

What deceives many of the less understanding and less thoughtful sort of people in this matter seems to be this. The Scripture is the word of God, and has nothing wrong in it, but is pure and perfect, and therefore those experiences which come from the Scripture must be right. But then it should be considered that affections may arise on the #emph[occasion] of the Scripture and not properly come #emph[from] the Scripture, as the genuine fruit of the Scripture and by a right use of it, but from an abuse of it. All that can be argued from the purity and perfection of the word of God, with respect to experiences, is this: those experiences which are #emph[agreeable] to the word of God are right and cannot be otherwise. It does not follow that those affections must be right which arise #emph[on the occasion] of the word of God coming to the mind.

What evidence is there that the devil cannot bring texts of Scripture to the mind and misapply them to deceive persons? There seems to be nothing in this which exceeds the power of #emph[Satan.] Bringing sounds or letters to persons’ minds is not a work of such mighty power that we have any reason to suppose nothing short of omnipotence can be enough for it. If #emph[Satan] has power to bring any words or sounds at all to persons’ minds, he may have power to bring words contained in the Bible. No higher sort of power is required in men to make the sounds that express the words of a text of Scripture than to make the sounds that express the words of an idle story or song. And so the same power in #emph[Satan] which is enough to renew one of those kinds of sounds in the mind is enough to renew the other. The difference in meaning, which depends wholly on custom, does not alter the case as to the ability to make or revive the sounds or letters. Or will anyone suppose that texts of Scripture are such sacred things that the devil would not dare to abuse them or touch them? In this also they are mistaken. He who was bold enough to lay hold of Christ himself and carry him here and there, into the wilderness, onto a high mountain, and to a pinnacle of the temple, is not afraid to touch the Scripture and abuse it for his own purposes. He showed this at the very time he was so bold with Christ, for he then brought one Scripture and another to deceive and tempt him. And if #emph[Satan] presumed, and was permitted, to put Christ himself in mind of texts of Scripture to tempt #emph[him,] what reason do we have to decide that he does not dare, or will not be permitted, to put wicked men in mind of texts of Scripture to tempt and deceive #emph[them?] And if #emph[Satan] may abuse one text of Scripture in this way, he may abuse another. Its being a very excellent place of Scripture, a comforting and precious promise, does not alter the case as to his boldness or his ability. And if he can bring one comforting text to the mind, he may bring a thousand. He may choose out such Scriptures as most serve his purpose. And he may heap up promises of Scripture which, according to the perverse application he makes of them, tend wonderfully to remove the rising doubts and to confirm the false joy and confidence of a poor deluded sinner.

We know that the devil’s instruments, corrupt and heretical teachers, can and do distort the Scripture to their own and others’ damnation (2 Pet 3:16). We see they have the free use of Scripture, every part of it. There is no text so precious and sacred that they are not permitted to abuse it to the eternal ruin of multitudes of souls. And there are no weapons they use with which they do more execution. And there is no reason of any kind to decide that the devil is not permitted to use the Scripture in this way, as well as his instruments. For when the latter do it, they do it as his instruments and servants, and by his prompting and influence. And no doubt he does himself what he prompts others to do. The devil’s servants only follow their master and do the same work he does himself.

And as the devil can abuse the Scripture to deceive and destroy men, so may men’s own folly and corruptions as well. The sin that is in men acts like its father. Men’s own hearts are deceitful like the devil and use the same means to deceive.

So it is evident that persons may have high affections of hope and joy arising on the occasion of texts of Scripture, indeed precious promises of Scripture, coming suddenly and remarkably to their minds as though they were spoken to them, indeed a great multitude of such texts following one another in a wonderful way. And yet all this may be no argument that these affections are divine, or that they are anything other than the effects of #emph[Satan’s] delusions.

And I would further observe that persons may have raised and joyful affections which may come with the word of God, and not only with it but from it, and those affections may not be from #emph[Satan,] nor properly from the corruptions of their own hearts, but from some influence of the Spirit of God with the word, and yet have nothing of the nature of true and saving religion in them. So the hearers on stony ground had great joy from the word, a joy which is represented as arising from the word as growth from a seed. And their affections had in their appearance a very great and exact resemblance to those represented by the growth on the good ground, the difference not appearing until it was shown by what followed, in a time of trial. And yet there was no saving religion in these affections.#footnote[Mr. #emph[Stoddard,] in his #emph[Guide to Christ,] speaks of it as a #emph[common thing] for persons, while in a natural condition and before they have ever truly #emph[accepted Christ,] to have #emph[promises] of Scripture come to them with #emph[a great deal of refreshing,] which they #emph[take as tokens of God’s love, and hope that God has accepted them,] and so they are confident of their good state. Page 8, 9. Impression #emph[anno] 1735.]
